ax” doesn't mean “no taxes.” The full picture is messier — and more interesting — than a single rate. Here's what actually matters when you compare states.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-lg bg-muted/20 p-4\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-percent h-4 w-4 text-emerald-400\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"line\",\"1x9vlm\",{\"x1\":\"19\",\"x2\":\"5\",\"y1\":\"5\",\"y2\":\"19\"}],[\"$\",\"circle\",\"4mh3h7\",{\"cx\":\"6.5\",\"cy\":\"6.5\",\"r\":\"2.5\"}],[\"$\",\"circle\",\"1mdrzq\",{\"cx\":\"17.5\",\"cy\":\"17.5\",\"r\":\"2.5\"}],\"$undefined\"]}],[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground\",\"children\":\"Income Tax Rates\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"This is the sexy number everyone talks about. And sure, 0% income tax in Texas or Florida can save you thousands versus California's 13.3% top rate. But it's just one piece of the puzzle — and sometimes a misleading one.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-lg bg-muted/20 p-4\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-house h-4 w-4 text-emerald-400\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"path\",\"5wwlr5\",{\"d\":\"M15 21v-8a1 1 0 0 0-1-1h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v8\"}],[\"$\",\"path\",\"1d0kgt\",{\"d\":\"M3 10a2 2 0 0 1 .709-1.528l7-5.999a2 2 0 0 1 2.582 0l7 5.999A2 2 0 0 1 21 10v9a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2z\"}],\"$undefined\"]}],[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground\",\"children\":\"Property Tax Burden\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"States with no income tax don't just magically have lower taxes — they get their money somewhere else. Texas, for example, hits you with some of the highest property tax rates in the country (averaging ~1.71%). California's Proposition 13 caps rates much lower (~0.71%), but the homes cost three times as much. It's a trade-off, and whether you win or lose depends heavily on whether you rent or own.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-lg bg-muted/20 p-4\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-receipt h-4 w-4 text-emerald-400\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[\"$L42\",\"$L43\",\"$L44\",\"$undefined\"]}],\"$L45\"]}],\"$L46\"]}],\"$L47\",\"$L48\",\"$L49\"]}]]}]]}]\n26:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-3 mb-6\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-book-open h-5 w-5 text-emerald-400\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"path\",\"1akyts\",{\"d\":\"M12 7v14\"}],[\"$\",\"path\",\"ruj8y\",{\"d\":\"M3 18a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 4 4 0 0 1 4-4h5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-6a3 3 0 0 0-3 3 3 3 0 0 0-3-3z\"}],\"$undefined\"]}]}],[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground\",\"children\":\"State Tax Comparison FAQ\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"details\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"summary\",null,{\"className\":\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-3 p-5 text-left font-medium text-foreground hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm sm:text-base\",\"children\":\"Which state has the lowest taxes?\"}],[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-chevron-down h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground transition-transform group-open:rotate-180\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"path\",\"qrunsl\",{\"d\":\"m6 9 6 6 6-6\"}],\"$undefined\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-5 pb-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"Among the states we cover, Texas and Florida have no state income tax, so they win on that front. But here's the thing: Texas has much higher property taxes than Florida. When you factor in all taxes — income, property, and sales — Florida generally comes out ahead for most income levels. That said, Wyoming, Alaska, and Nevada (which we don't cover yet) rank among the lowest-tax states nationally. For the five states we do cover, Florida is usually the winner.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"details\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"summary\",null,{\"className\":\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-3 p-5 text-left font-medium text-foreground hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm sm:text-base\",\"children\":\"Should I move to a state with no income tax?\"}],[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-chevron-down h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground transition-transform group-open:rotate-180\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"path\",\"qrunsl\",{\"d\":\"m6 9 6 6 6-6\"}],\"$undefined\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-5 pb-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"It depends on your full financial picture — and we mean full. States with no income tax make up the revenue elsewhere: higher property taxes, sales taxes, or fees. Texas has no income tax but hits you with some of the highest property taxes in the US. If you're a renter with a good salary, Texas or Florida is very attractive. If you own an expensive home, the property tax bill might offset your income tax savings. Run the numbers for your specific situation before making any decisions.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"details\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[\"$L4a\",\"$L4b\"]}],\"$L4c\",\"$L4d\"]}]]}]\n27:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-16 rounded-2xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 p-8 text-center\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground\",\"child